+---
+node: record-store
+---
+
+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers correct the same plot visit independently. Tablets can be offline for 48 hours, so two corrections often start from the same copy of a visit and reach the server days apart, in an order that says nothing about which worker is right. The record store has to say what the visit's current content is when that happens.
+
+Every option keeps both original submissions; none may discard one. The options differ in what the record store does next: hold the two copies for a person to settle, or combine them itself.
+
+The answer is hard to change later. Tablet-sync sizes its local storage and retry behavior around the submission metadata the policy requires. Review-export sizes its coordinator screen and its rule for which visits enter the Friday CSV around how unsettled work is represented. A wrong choice is paid for in all three nodes, and the cost of one kind of wrong choice is invisible: a combined record that looks clean and is scientifically inconsistent reaches the regional science partner without anyone having been asked.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Weighted criteria:
+
+- **Unnoticed combination of related edits — major.** Some fields of a visit depend on each other in ways their names do not show: a revised species identification and its cover estimate, an observer list and the notes that refer to it. A policy that joins edits by field can pair one worker's identification with another's estimate. Importance comes from the tenet *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* and from the non-goal of inferring scientific intent.
+- **Rules to maintain — major.** Whatever logic decides how edits combine has to be written, tested, and revised when the visit's fields change. Importance comes from the tenet *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*.
+- **Coordinator review workload — major.** Two coordinators settle everything the record store does not. The same tenet applies, pulling the other way: a queue two people cannot clear is also a workflow they cannot operate.
+- **Visits ready for the Friday export — moderate.** A visit awaiting review is missing from that week's CSV. Importance comes from the goal of making reviewable problems visible before the Friday export.
+- **Tablet storage and submission size — minor.** At about 6,000 visits a season on 12 tablets, neither option strains a device; the criterion separates the options only slightly.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each edit is a complete copy of the visit and names the revision it started from. When two copies start from the same revision, the record store keeps both as competing revisions and marks the visit unresolved. A coordinator compares them and settles the visit. The record store never looks inside the scientific fields to decide an outcome.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A fetches revision r1, corrects an observer's name, and submits r2. Worker B, also holding r1, adds a missing species and submits r3. The visit is unresolved with r2 and r3 competing, even though the two edits touch different fields, until a coordinator settles it.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Unnoticed combination of related edits: **strong**. Nothing is combined without a coordinator seeing both complete copies.
+- Rules to maintain: **strong**. The only rule is whether two revisions share a starting revision; it does not depend on the visit's fields.
+- Coordinator review workload: **weak**. Every pair of concurrent edits needs review, including edits to unrelated fields that a person would combine without hesitation.
+- Visits ready for the Friday export: **weak**. Every concurrently edited visit is held out of the CSV until reviewed.
+- Tablet storage and submission size: **strong**. A tablet keeps one revision ID per visit and sends the visit it already holds.
+
+**Summary.** Trades coordinator time and export readiness for certainty that no record is combined by rule.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each edit supplies the state it started from and the changes it makes. The record store compares both edits with their common starting state. Changes to different fields are combined automatically into the current visit; changes to the same field go to a coordinator, who sees only the overlap. Each field of the combined visit records which submission it came from.
+
+**Demonstration.** In the same scenario, the record store combines A's observer correction and B's added species into one current visit with no review, and the visit is in Friday's CSV. If instead A revises a species identification while B revises that species' cover estimate, the two changes are to different fields and are also combined with no review.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- Unnoticed combination of related edits: **weak**. Disjoint field changes are joined whether or not the fields are scientifically related; relationships the rules do not encode pass unreviewed.
+- Rules to maintain: **weak**. Combination needs separate rules for scalar fields, the observer list, and species entries (what counts as the same entry, how reordering and removal combine), and each rule changes with the visit's fields.
+- Coordinator review workload: **strong**. Only overlapping changes reach a coordinator, and the review covers the overlapping fields, not the whole visit.
+- Visits ready for the Friday export: **strong**. Visits with only disjoint edits are current as soon as both edits arrive.
+- Tablet storage and submission size: **partial**. A tablet keeps a full base snapshot per edited visit and sends structured changes; workable at this scale but more state to keep correct across restarts.
+
+**Summary.** Trades rule upkeep and the chance of an unreviewed inconsistent record for a short review queue and fuller exports.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions.
+
+The two options split the major criteria cleanly, so the choice is about which failure the team would rather live with. A long review queue is visible: coordinators see it, can count it, and can tell the partner which visits are held. A merge rule that joins related edits fails silently: the record is current, exported, and wrong, and nothing prompts anyone to look. The decision turns on the tenet *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*, and it honors the non-goal of inferring scientific intent, since any field-level merge rule is a standing claim about which fields are scientifically independent. On the two-coordinator tenet the options pull in opposite directions; the selection accepts more review work in exchange for no merge rules to maintain.
+
+**What it forecloses.** Edits to unrelated fields are not combined without a person. No visit becomes current as a mixture of two workers' submissions unless a coordinator made it so.
+
+**What tablet-sync has to accommodate.**
+
+- Keep, for each visit a tablet holds, the ID of the revision its copy started from, across restarts and multi-day disconnection, and send it with every edit.
+- Always submit the complete visit, not a set of changes.
+- Tell a worker when their submitted edit is competing with another revision and awaiting review, and show the competing revisions. Being sent to review is an expected outcome of a successful submission, not an error.
+- Take up a coordinator's settled revision as the new starting point for further edits.
+
+**What review-export has to accommodate.**
+
+- A screen that shows the competing revisions of a visit as complete visits side by side, with their authors and receipt times, and lets a coordinator settle the visit.
+- Review of visits whose competing edits touch unrelated fields, which will be most of the queue; the screen should make such cases quick to settle.
+- An export that holds unresolved visits out of the CSV, and a way for coordinators to see before Friday which visits are being held.
+
+**When the other approach is worth reconsidering.** Field-aware merge remains a credible design, and it answers exactly the weakness accepted here. Reconsider it if the queue regularly holds more than two coordinators can clear before the Friday export, if most reviewed conflicts turn out to be unrelated-field edits that coordinators settle the same way every time, or if the partner objects to the number of visits held out of the CSV. Because the record store keeps every revision complete along with the revision it started from, the three inputs a merge needs are already retained; adopting merge later would add merge rules, field-level provenance, and an overlap review view, without re-collecting past submissions. A narrower step is also available before a full merge: automatically settling a small, named set of cases the team has judged safe.
