-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+The author comes from authentication, not from the envelope. A submission always carries the complete visit; the record store accepts no partial or field-level changes.
+
+### Acceptance
+
+Any authenticated field worker or coordinator may submit a revision to any visit. Before accepting, the record store validates field types and checks that the plot UUID is known to the site catalog; archived plots are accepted because historical visits still refer to them.
+
+An accepted submission becomes a new head:
+
+| Base revision | Outcome |
+|---|---|
+| Absent, and the visit does not exist | The visit is created with this revision as its only head |
+| A current head | The new revision takes that head's place; the other heads, if any, are unchanged |
+| A revision of the visit that is no longer a head | The new revision is added as another head |
+| Absent, and the visit already exists | The new revision is added as another head |
+
+Acceptance decisions for one visit are applied one at a time. Two submissions naming the same base therefore always end as two competing revisions, whichever arrives first; arrival order never selects a winner.
+
+A worker may keep editing a visit that is unresolved. A submission based on one of the competing revisions replaces that revision in the head set, and the visit stays unresolved.
+
+### Rejection
+
+A rejected submission is not stored as a revision and changes no visit. The response names one of these reasons so the tablet can keep the draft and tell the worker what happened:
+
+| Reason | Condition |
+|---|---|
+| `unauthenticated` | The caller is not authenticated |
+| `invalid_fields` | A field is missing or has the wrong type; the response lists the fields |
+| `unknown_plot` | The plot UUID is not in the site catalog |
+| `unknown_base` | The base revision ID is not a revision of the named visit |
+| `key_reused` | The idempotency key was accepted before with a different envelope or a different author |
+
+### Retries
+
+A retry with an accepted idempotency key, the same author, and an identical envelope returns the original acceptance result and stores nothing. The original result reports the revision ID assigned then; the visit's state in the response is its state now, which may have changed since.
+
+### Acceptance response
+
+An acceptance response carries the assigned revision ID, the visit's state (`settled` or `unresolved`), and the IDs of its head revisions.
+
+## Retrieval
+
+| Operation | Returns |
+|---|---|
+| Get visit | State; when `settled`, the current revision in full; when `unresolved`, every competing revision in full and no current revision |
+| Get visit history | Every revision of the visit, with its kind (worker or resolution), parents, author, receipt time, and fields |
+| List visits | Visit IDs with state and head revision IDs, filterable by state |
+
+A revision returned in full includes its revision ID, parents, author, receipt time, and visit fields. Retrieval requires authentication and is open to field workers and coordinators alike.
+
+Review-export decides export eligibility; the record store only reports state. An unresolved visit offers no current revision for an export to use.
+
+## Resolution
+
+Only a coordinator may resolve an unresolved visit. A resolution submission carries:
+
+| Field | Content |
+|---|---|
+| Idempotency key | As for worker submissions |
+| Visit ID | The unresolved visit |
+| Resolved revision IDs | The competing revisions the coordinator reviewed |
+| Visit fields | The complete visit the coordinator settles on |
+
+The visit fields may repeat one competing revision unchanged or be a copy the coordinator has composed from several. The record store applies the same field validation as for worker submissions and does not check the fields against the competing revisions; combining observations is the coordinator's judgment.
+
+The resolved revision IDs must equal the visit's head set at the moment of acceptance. If a competing revision has arrived or another coordinator has resolved the visit since the coordinator fetched it, the record store rejects the resolution with `stale_resolution` and returns the current head set, so no revision is resolved without having been reviewed. A caller who is not a coordinator is rejected with `not_coordinator`.
+
+An accepted resolution becomes a resolution revision whose parents are the resolved revisions and whose author is the coordinator. It is the visit's only head, so the visit is `settled`. The competing revisions it resolved remain in the visit's history.
+
+A worker submission that arrives later with a base that the resolution replaced is added as another head, and the visit becomes unresolved again.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrent visit edits | Whole-visit revisions: competing complete revisions go to a coordinator | Field-aware three-way merge: combine disjoint field changes automatically and review only overlaps | Merge rules can combine scientifically related edits (a species identification and its cover estimate) without noticing, and they must be maintained for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. A larger review queue is the accepted cost; edits to unrelated fields also need review. Full reasoning, downstream obligations, and when to reconsider: [decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md](decisions/concurrent-visit-edits.md). |
+| Visit state | Derived from the head set | A stored status flag updated by each operation | One rule covers first submissions, stale bases, edits during review, and edits after resolution, and state cannot disagree with the revisions. |
+| Current revision while unresolved | None | Keep serving the last revision accepted before the edits diverged | The earlier revision is one both workers have already corrected; offering it as current would put a known-superseded value in front of exports and tablets. |
+| Resolution content | A complete visit authored by the coordinator, which may combine competing revisions | Coordinator may only pick one competing revision | Edits to unrelated fields are the common case; pick-one would force a coordinator to drop one worker's correction from the current visit and then re-enter it as a further edit. |
+| Stale resolution | Rejected when the resolved IDs differ from the head set | Accept and leave unreviewed heads competing; accept and drop unreviewed heads | A revision must not be settled away by a coordinator who never saw it, and two coordinators working the same visit must not overwrite each other. |
+| Edits to an unresolved visit | Accepted; they advance one competing revision | Reject worker submissions until the visit is resolved | Field workers keep working regardless of the review queue; a rejected correction would sit only on a shared tablet. |
+| Revision storage | Complete visit fields per revision | Store field-level differences against the base | About 6,000 visits a season makes full copies negligible, and a complete copy is the submitted payload itself. |
+
+The record store compares neither field values nor payloads when deciding whether revisions compete: two revisions from the same base compete even when their fields are identical.
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. How a tablet learns that visits it holds have changed on the server (polling List visits, or a change cursor) is settled with the tablet-sync design.
+2. Two workers who record the same plot visit on different tablets create two visits with different IDs. The record store treats them as separate visits; detecting such duplicates is not part of concurrency handling.
+3. Withdrawing or deleting a visit is not supported.
